# Binary fixtures must never have their line endings rewritten.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# Without it, git applies text normalisation by guess, and Windows checkouts
# (core.autocrlf=true, the default on the GitHub Windows runner) rewrite LF to
# CRLF inside files it decides are text.
#
# For a PDF that is fatal in a specific, quiet way: the cross-reference table
# stores ABSOLUTE BYTE OFFSETS. Inserting one byte per line shifts every object
# in the file, so every offset in the xref becomes wrong. The document either
# fails to open or opens only because the parser reconstructs the xref — which
# means a fixture written to exercise a precise structural path silently stops
# exercising it.
#
# Found by Test (Windows) on 2026-08-03: hybrid-xrefstm-revision-probe.pdf, a
# fixture built to pin #872 (/XRefStm hybrid-reference handling), passed on
# macOS and Linux and failed on Windows. 33 other checked-in PDFs were exposed
# to the same corruption; they had not failed visibly, which is worse rather
# than better — a corpus fixture that quietly stops testing what it was written
# for is coverage loss you cannot see.

*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.icc   binary
*.gz    binary
*.zip   binary
*.jar   binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.dylib binary
*.so    binary
*.dll   binary

# Shell scripts must keep LF even on Windows checkouts — a CRLF shebang line
# makes the interpreter fail with a confusing "bad interpreter" error.
*.sh    text eol=lf
